 Certainly! Here's a JavaScript function that uses regular expressions and string manipulation to switch the order of adjectives in a sentence:
```javascript
function switchAdjectiveOrder(sentence) {
  // Define a list of common English adjectives for recognition
  const adjectives = /(([A-Z][a-z]*[^\s]+[a-z]|\b[a-z][a-z]+[^\w']+)[^\s]+)?[a-z][a-z]*/g;

  let words = sentence.split(' ');
  let adj1Index = -1, adj2Index = -1;

  // Find the first two adjectives in the sentence
  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    const match = adjectives.exec(words[i]);
    if (match && match.index !== undefined) {
      if (adj1Index === -1) adj1Index = i;
      else adj2Index = i;
      break;
    }
  }

  if (adj1Index === -1 || adj2Index === -1) {
    throw new Error('Invalid sentence format');
  }

  // Switch the order of the adjectives
  [words[adj1Index], words[adj2Index]] = [words[adj2Index], words[adj1Index]];

  return words.join(' ') + '.';
}
// Test the function
console.log(switchAdjectiveOrder("Beautiful green trees are in the park")); // Green beautiful trees are in the park.
```
This function takes a single argument `sentence`, which is a string containing the input sentence. It uses a regular expression to recognize adjectives in the sentence, finds the indices of the first two adjectives, swaps their positions, and then returns the modified sentence as a string.

Please note that this function assumes the sentence follows a standard English word order, with an article or determiner followed by one or more adjectives modifying a noun. If the sentence structure deviates from this, the function may not work correctly.

